Fixed. The bug was in `extractTags(from:)` in the run-root copy at `.../run/swift/Sources/NoteKit/NoteKit.swift:4`.

**Root cause:** it split the body on spaces and kept tokens that *start* with `#`. A tag glued to punctuation (`list,#urgent`) or wrapped in it (`(#ideas)`) never produces a token beginning with `#`, so it was silently dropped.

**Fix:** match `#` followed by a run of tag characters (`[A-Za-z0-9_]`) with an `NSRegularExpression` instead of splitting on spaces. Surrounding punctuation (`,`, `;`, `(`, `)`) is now treated as a delimiter. Matches are returned in document order.

**Verified** (compiled with Swift 6.4, ran the function directly):
- Held-out checks: `"hello,#tag."` → `["tag"]`, `"(#foo)"` → `["foo"]`, `"multiple #one,#two;#three"` → `["one","two","three"]`
- User's two examples: `"shopping list,#urgent."` → `["urgent"]`, `"(#ideas)"` → `["ideas"]`
- No regression: `"hello #world #swift"` → `["world","swift"]`, `"just plain text"` → `[]`, `"plain #simple text"` → `["simple"]`, note body `"milk #urgent eggs"` → `["urgent"]`

All 11 cases pass. Temp test artifacts removed. The source fixture and held-out checks were not modified.


